> Added in prompt v4 from R004 evidence; v4 takes effect at the R005 run boundary, and R004 is assessed under v3. Trigger references quote the journal heading time exactly as written:
> 
> - Before opening a PR, run the repository's required regression gate (the same command as --verify) in a fresh clone checked out at the exact commit. For an unpushed commit, clone from the local repository. Record the commit, the pass, fail and skip counts, and the reason for each skip. A skip the repository does not document as needing local data is a finding. A failure is a finding, fixed through Howl review and acceptance or paused under section 17. The controller does not hand-edit or regenerate product files to make the clone pass. A clone on this machine still shares its installed tools; the CI rule below covers that. (Trigger: 2026-10-10T16:26:38Z, 2026-10-10 20:54 UTC.)
> - The session verification command is a short command that runs the repository's required gate unchanged: an existing one such as `make test-full`, or a committed script such as `sh scripts/gate.sh`. Record its content hash in the journal. If a session creates or edits the script, the independent review confirms it is not narrower than the required gate, and any narrowing is a finding. The --verify-timeout rule above still applies. This avoids exact-literal permission mismatches on inline shell chains; it is not known to prevent every provider's shell refusals (CUBS-P-011, provisional). (Trigger: 2026-10-10 19:49 UTC, 2026-10-10 20:39 UTC.)
> - Target every PR at the default branch. Stack a PR only when a later change cannot be built without an earlier unmerged one, and state that reason, its base and the merge order in its body. Retarget it to the default branch before handoff once its parent merges. A change merged into a non-default branch is not delivered: open a PR to the default branch whose tree is identical to the verified commit. Record MERGED_VERIFIED only when the change is on the default branch and the gate passes on a fresh clone of it, with skip counts recorded. (Trigger: 2026-10-10 22:30 UTC; also R003 PRs #8 and #9, runs/R003/FINAL-ASSESSMENT.md.)
> - Before handing a PR to the user, read the CI checks for its head commit. A failing or pending check is a finding. Fix it through Howl, and count the fix session against the run's session bound; if none remains, pause under section 17. Never describe a PR with a failing check as ready. If CI is absent or unreadable, say so in the handoff. (Trigger: 2026-10-10 22:30 UTC.)
> - For user-interface work, the controller itself (not a local model) inspects screenshots at every viewport the tests cover, before recording the run outcome and preferably before Howl acceptance, so that findings can enter a goal. This adds to Howl review and acceptance and does not replace them. Record the screenshot paths, viewports and findings in the assessment. A readability problem is a finding, fixed through Howl within bounds or listed as open, and is never counted as passed. (Trigger: 2026-10-10 21:40 UTC.)
> - Before a session that would change an input the run contract (runs/<run-id>/RUN-CONTRACT.md) lists as reused or unchanged, pause under section 17 for a dated user decision, then record the amendment quoting it. Keep the original text on record and report the change as a deviation. After each such session, diff the protected paths; any change outside the approved amendment is a finding and a pause. This does not relax the v3 label-freeze rule or section 18. (Trigger: 2026-10-10T16:27:03Z, 2026-10-10 20:39 UTC.)
> - A HowlPlane session that repairs a Howl defect counts against the run's session bound unless the user's decision authorizing the repair says otherwise. Record which applies when it starts. (Trigger: 2026-10-10 22:30 UTC; R004 ran two repair sessions beyond its six-session bound.)
